WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:04.020
Will we ever see more of the Justice&nbsp;
Society? Will everything we just saw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:08.400
in Black Adam be undone when the Flash movie&nbsp;
finally drops? Does the Rock shave his head,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:12.844
or is he just bald? Only one of those is a&nbsp;
question we won't try to answer in this video.

00:00:13.033 --> 00:00:14.935
Warning — major spoilers ahead.

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:18.720
One of the craziest things about the "Black&nbsp;
Adam" film is that the character — who,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:22.620
while he certainly has his fans, has been&nbsp;
at best a C-tier character for most of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:26.700
run — is getting his own solo film, despite&nbsp;
the fact that he was created specifically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:31.980
as an antagonist and foil to Billy Batson's&nbsp;
Shazam — formerly Captain Marvel. Even Venom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:36.240
and Joker got to star alongside Spider-Man and&nbsp;
Batman before getting their own solo films.

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:40.440
In fact, Black Adam was supposed to be&nbsp;
in the original 2019 "Shazam!" film;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:44.160
however, The Rock later revealed how&nbsp;
he felt that script was too crammed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:48.780
and forced DC and Warner Bros. to make his&nbsp;
solo "Black Adam" film first instead. But now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:53.460
with this movie's Superman post-credit tease, when&nbsp;
will Black Adam fight his actual arch-nemesis?

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:56.880
It's even crazier, considering there's&nbsp;
already going to be an upcoming "Shazam!"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:01.260
sequel — 2023's "Shazam! Fury of the Gods"&nbsp;
— where the villains are the daughters of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:05.461
Atlas — played by Helen Mirren and Lucy&nbsp;
Liu — and not The Rock's Black Adam.

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:09.300
We've already mentioned it, but it bears&nbsp;
repeating: What's probably gotten fans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:13.140
buzzing most when walking out of the "Black&nbsp;
Adam" film is the post-credit scene featuring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:17.640
none other than the Man of Steel himself,&nbsp;
Superman, played once again by the DCEU's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:22.320
Henry Cavill. This teaser scene even implies that&nbsp;
a showdown may occur between the two super titans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:26.100
considering Amanda Waller essentially tells&nbsp;
Black Adam that while he's allowed to remain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:30.120
free to protect Kahndaq, he will also now&nbsp;
be forced to stay there "like a prison,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:34.920
or else there will be consequences. Superman&nbsp;
seems to be those aforementioned consequences.

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:39.360
While it seems out-of-character for Superman to&nbsp;
be so willing to work for someone like Waller,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:43.140
the idea of the two super beings facing&nbsp;
off in an epic brawl could potentially be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:46.920
fun — despite the fact that it would make&nbsp;
more sense for it to be Shazam instead.

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:51.360
Obviously Superman would win, and he has in the&nbsp;
comics when they've gone up against each other.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:55.980
That is, unless The Rock still has his alleged&nbsp;
"can't lose a fight" clause in his contract,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.980 --> 00:02:00.420
like the one from the "Fast and Furious"&nbsp;
films. Sure, Superman is indeed vulnerable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:04.800
to magic -- from which Black Adam and Shazam's&nbsp;
powers are derived — but it doesn't straight up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:09.120
weaken him like Kryptonite does. It's more that&nbsp;
Superman doesn't have any defense against it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:12.692
meaning magical lightning will hurt&nbsp;
him, while regular lightning doesn't.

00:02:12.692 --> 00:02:17.640
Many comic book superheroes have very convoluted&nbsp;
— even contradictory — origin stories,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:21.900
particularly those that originated in the&nbsp;
early Golden Age era of comic books and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:25.740
lasted into the modern era. For instance,&nbsp;
Superman didn't even originally fly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:30.540
and the original Green Lantern's initial&nbsp;
weakness was wood! However, very few heroes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:35.820
have more convoluted and contradicting origins&nbsp;
than DC Comics' Hawkman. Debuting in 1940,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:40.140
Hawkman's initial origin was that he was Carter&nbsp;
Hall, an archaeologist who turned out to be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:43.800
resurrected Egyptian prince who used armor&nbsp;
and weapons made out of a magical metal.

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:47.160
Then, when the Silver Age came&nbsp;
around in the 1950s and '60s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:52.140
most of the Golden Age superheroes were either&nbsp;
phased out or given a sci-fi makeover. Hawkman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:56.340
was no exception. He went from having a&nbsp;
mystical — and pretty messed up — origin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.340 --> 00:03:00.420
of being a white re-incarnated Egyptian&nbsp;
prince, to being an alien space cop.

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:05.100
Having said all that, it's never really clear in&nbsp;
the "Black Adam" film what his origin actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:10.380
is. That's a little strange, considering we at&nbsp;
least get tidbits from most of the other JSA&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:14.520
members about where their powers came from.&nbsp;
To be fair, since his name is Carter Hall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:18.720
it's unlikely he's the Thanagarian space cop&nbsp;
version. But that doesn't mean he couldn't be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:22.860
a mix of the various versions of the character.&nbsp;
For instance, the Golden Age version didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:27.660
have a super high-tech jet either — you know,&nbsp;
because he has wings and flying is basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:31.980
his main thing — so it's an open-ended&nbsp;
question for now. This especially true,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:36.180
considering the comic books eventually merged&nbsp;
the two Hawkman origins — with him being both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:40.920
a reincarnated Egyptian prince and finding a&nbsp;
crashed Thanagarian space ship where he discovers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:46.020
the mystical metal that grants him his powers.&nbsp;
Again, even for comics, this stuff is complicated.

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:49.560
Hopefully you've heeded the warning&nbsp;
about spoilers from the intro,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:52.740
because here's a big one: during&nbsp;
the climax of the "Black Adam" film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:57.420
in a desperate battle with the uber-powerful&nbsp;
demon Sabbac, Pierce Brosnan's Dr. Fate is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.420 --> 00:04:01.980
violently and thoroughly killed. This happens&nbsp;
once it becomes clear to Fate that the JSA is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:06.384
no match for Sabbac, and that his premonitions&nbsp;
show his friend Hawkman dying in the onslaught.

00:04:06.384 --> 00:04:08.334
"My vision has shown me the future."

00:04:08.334 --> 00:04:12.420
Thus, Fate uses himself as a distraction&nbsp;
to desperately hold off Sabbac while,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:17.160
at the same time, he astral projects to the&nbsp;
captive Black Adam to make him say "Shazam!" once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:21.900
more and regain his powers. Fate realizes that&nbsp;
Adam is the only one who can defeat the demon.

00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:27.060
However, Kent Nelson isn't the only Dr. Fate&nbsp;
found in the pages of DC Comics. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:30.060
there have been many people who took on&nbsp;
the "Dr. Fate" name throughout the years.

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:34.800
This includes the most recent — and current&nbsp;
fan-favorite — Khalid Nassour, who actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:39.060
has an Egyptian background, unlike the other&nbsp;
versions. But even before that, there was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:43.980
the extreme 90's grunge version, who was simply&nbsp;
called "Fate," who didn't even wear the helmet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:48.720
but definitely had every Nirvana cassette he could&nbsp;
find. There were also two different women — named&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:53.040
Linda Strauss and Inza Nelson who took on the&nbsp;
role of Dr. Fate in the late '80s and early '90s.

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:57.240
In other words, Kent Nelson's death doesn't&nbsp;
necessarily mean the end of Dr. Fate as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.240 --> 00:05:01.440
character in the DCEU. Furthermore, this&nbsp;
doesn't discount prequel films with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:05.580
long-lived Nelson in his previous adventures,&nbsp;
only alluded to in "Black Adam" itself.

00:05:05.869 --> 00:05:07.980
One of the issues "Black Adam" inadvertently&nbsp;
brings up: If the Justice Society of America&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.980 --> 00:05:11.640
has been around all along — with Dr.&nbsp;
Fate implying that he and Hawkman had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:15.180
been a team for at least a few decades —&nbsp;
then why haven't they helped with any of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:18.420
the "end of the world" scenarios that&nbsp;
have cropped up throughout the DCEU?

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:21.540
Couldn't the JSA have helped&nbsp;
with Darkseid's Parademons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:25.740
or the evil General Zod in Metropolis,&nbsp;
or the destructive Doomsday in Gotham?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:29.040
It feels especially weird, considering the&nbsp;
Justice League seemed to be a whole new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:32.400
concept without the precedent that would&nbsp;
have been set by the Justice Society.

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:36.360
This is admittedly a problem in all&nbsp;
interconnected superhero universes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:41.940
and has been a questionable element of the MCU as&nbsp;
well. Worse, unlike The Suicide Squad -- another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:47.160
superhuman group in the DCEU -- the JSA are&nbsp;
supposed to be out-in-the-open heroes. Not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.160 --> 00:05:52.260
a great look for a supposedly virtuous superhero&nbsp;
team, especially considering the pretty inhumane&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.260 --> 00:05:57.000
metahuman prison revealed at the end of the second&nbsp;
act, where they send the de-powered Black Adam.

00:05:57.360 --> 00:06:01.620
It's been reported that after Ezra Miller's&nbsp;
apology for their past abusive behavior,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:05.760
they have been participating in re-shoots for&nbsp;
the upcoming "The Flash" film. The solo Flash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:09.840
film is expected to be a retelling of the&nbsp;
"Flashpoint" comic book event from 2011,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:14.160
which was about The Flash using the Speed Force&nbsp;
to go back in time to stop his mother's murder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:18.840
and prevent his father from being framed for it.&nbsp;
Unfortunately, his time-traveling escapades cause&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.840 --> 00:06:24.120
grave consequences for the entire DC multiverse,&nbsp;
which includes Aquaman's Atlanteans and Wonder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.120 --> 00:06:29.460
Woman's Amazons going to war. In fact, the&nbsp;
"Flashpoint" event was used to create the "New 52"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.460 --> 00:06:34.860
universe — similar to 1985's "Crisis on Infinite&nbsp;
Earths"...though not nearly as successful.

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:38.880
It's clear that things would necessarily&nbsp;
be different for a live-action adaptation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:42.240
but it's also clear from what footage&nbsp;
we have seen in the first trailer for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:45.240
"The Flash" that at least a few of&nbsp;
the core plot elements will remain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:49.200
intact. Assuming it ever comes out,&nbsp;
the movie would also introduce actress&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:54.000
Sasha Calle as the DCEU's Supergirl and&nbsp;
re-introduce Michael Keaton as Batman.

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:57.660
What does this all have to do with "Black&nbsp;
Adam" and its unanswered questions?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.660 --> 00:07:02.640
Well, "Black Adam" has been over a decade in the&nbsp;
making. But with incoming Warner Bros. Discovery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.640 --> 00:07:07.680
CEO David Zaslav announcing an intention&nbsp;
to revamp the DCEU — and to continue moving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:11.700
forward with "The Flash" — that means anything&nbsp;
can happen in the meantime. That's especially true&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:15.960
considering that the "Flashpoint" storyline was&nbsp;
designed specifically to streamline continuity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.960 --> 00:07:20.217
and there's no guarantee anything in "Black&nbsp;
Adam" will remain canonical going forward.

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:26.160
In the "Black Adam" solo film, the main attraction&nbsp;
is, spoilers again, Black Adam. Despite that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:29.580
the heroes of the Justice Society&nbsp;
of America — consisting of Hawkman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.580 --> 00:07:33.240
Dr. Fate, Cyclone, and Atom Smasher —&nbsp;
might've actually stolen the thunder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:36.420
from the film's own protagonist.&nbsp;
That makes sense in some ways,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.420 --> 00:07:41.100
considering how much more colorful and bombastic&nbsp;
they are, in comparison to the broody Black Adam.

00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:44.755
"Cyclone. Smasher. Now is your time."

00:07:44.755 --> 00:07:49.260
Even better is that the core group dynamics are&nbsp;
already so well-defined — their interplay is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.260 --> 00:07:53.040
one of the best things about "Black Adam."&nbsp;
It's not necessarily groundbreaking stuff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:56.940
but you have the go-getter rookie with a chip&nbsp;
on his shoulder due to being a legacy hero,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.940 --> 00:08:01.140
a scientist hero who has a tragic backstory&nbsp;
but is trying to do good despite that fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.140 --> 00:08:05.280
and the rich benefactor who's a brave and&nbsp;
powerful leader in his own right. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:09.120
if they bring in any of the other Dr. Fate&nbsp;
variants, then the filmmakers can also play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:13.260
with that as a dynamic -- either being a group&nbsp;
of all green newcomers whipped into shape by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.260 --> 00:08:17.700
the tough-but-fair Hawkman, or Hawkman having&nbsp;
to come to terms with accepting a new Fate.

00:08:17.700 --> 00:08:21.060
Furthermore, the JSA existed&nbsp;
for decades in the comics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.060 --> 00:08:24.420
and there are many heroes who still haven't&nbsp;
gotten the big-budget, big-screen treatment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.420 --> 00:08:28.320
and still more who haven't even gotten a&nbsp;
live-action or animated treatment at all.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.320 --> 00:08:32.040
Who doesn't want to see some Spectre&nbsp;
or Alan Scott Green Lantern action?

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:37.860
A quick recap: The Justice Society of America&nbsp;
is sent in by Amanda Waller to go to Kahndaq&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.860 --> 00:08:42.060
and take Black Adam prisoner, due to his power&nbsp;
level supposedly being a threat to international&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.060 --> 00:08:48.120
stability. She contacts JSA leader Hawkman first,&nbsp;
and he brings in veteran superhero Dr. Fate and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.120 --> 00:08:53.400
two rookies. Cyclone, aka Maxine Hunkel, is a&nbsp;
super genius with the power of "aerokinesis".&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.400 --> 00:08:58.680
In other words, she manipulates wind. Like some&nbsp;
kind of hurricane or something. Atom Smasher,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.680 --> 00:09:04.260
aka Albert "Al" Rothstein, is the nephew of the&nbsp;
original Atom – played in a comedic cameo by Henry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.260 --> 00:09:08.520
Winkler – who has the power to manipulate his&nbsp;
atoms and grow exponentially in size and strength.

00:09:08.520 --> 00:09:09.801
"I get big. That's what I do."

00:09:09.801 --> 00:09:13.620
As the youngest and newest members, Cyclone&nbsp;
and Atom Smasher find an instant connection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.620 --> 00:09:17.820
based on that fact alone. However, as they&nbsp;
spend more time together — both in battle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.820 --> 00:09:22.200
and in safety on Hawkman's lavish jet -- they&nbsp;
seem to be developing more than just a friendly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.200 --> 00:09:26.340
bond. Of course, since the film has to focus&nbsp;
more on Black Adam's story than the JSA's,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.340 --> 00:09:28.980
their relationship is left up in&nbsp;
the air by the end of the film.

00:09:28.980 --> 00:09:33.420
In the comics, it doesn't seem like a canonical&nbsp;
relationship exists between the two heroes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.420 --> 00:09:35.460
but when has that stopped adaptations before?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.460 --> 00:09:39.660
Most fans likely wouldn't mind. Maybe&nbsp;
she can write him some love letters.

